<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibrahim al-Hamdi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1943">1943</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibrahim al-Hamdi (1943–1977) was president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> Arab Republic (North Yemen) from 1974 to 1977. He came to power in a bloodless military coup, curbed the power of the tribal sheikhs, promoted development through local cooperatives and sought closer ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">South Yemen</a>. He was assassinated in Sanaa in October 1977, shortly before a planned visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibrahim-al-hamdi.jpg" alt="Ibrahim al-Hamdi" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibrahim_al-Hamdi_during_parade_of_the_Yemen_Arab_republic%27s_army.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Al-Hamdi was born on 30 September 1943 in Qattab, in Ibb in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, of a family of the Banu Hamdan tribe. His father, a judge, taught him Islamic law, and after leaving the Aviation College without finishing his training as a pilot he worked beside his father in the court of Dhamar under Imam Ahmed. Under President Abdullah as-Sallal he commanded the commandos, took charge of the western, eastern and central provinces in 1972, and became Deputy Prime Minister for Internal Affairs and then a senior commander of the armed forces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>On 13 June 1974 he was among the officers who overthrew President Abdul Rahman al-Iryani in what they called a corrective movement, and he became the third president of the Yemen Arab Republic. Inspired by Nasserism, he set out to calm tribal feuds, end favouritism and bribery, and proclaim all Yemenis equal. He abolished the Ministry of Tribal Affairs in favour of a Ministry of Local Administration, restructured the army and raised military and civilian salaries.</p><p>A five-year plan paved roads and built schools and clinics, largely through Local Development Associations, community bodies that raised taxes and funds themselves. He gave 31 percent of the annual budget to education and began free breakfasts for pupils in remote areas. He also planned popular conventions to prepare for elections, built up defences at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bab-el-mandeb">Bab al-Mandab</a> and reorganised the army into four forces, the Giants under his brother Abdullah among them. His policies clashed with Saudi Arabia and the traditional elites.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 11 October 1977 he and his brother were killed at the Sanaa house of his vice president, Ahmad al-Ghashmi, two days before a visit to the south to negotiate unification. No official investigation was ever opened. Al-Ghashmi, the security chief Mohammad al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khami">Khamis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abdullah-saleh">Ali Abdullah Saleh</a> are believed to have conspired against him, and many assume a Saudi agent killed him. Two French women were also killed and their bodies placed with the brothers'. His memory was long suppressed, until protesters carried his portrait in the revolution against Saleh.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ibrahim al-Hamdi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>President <b>Ibrahim al-Hamdi</b> was assassinated in 1977, and his successor too, before Ali Abdullah Saleh took power in 1978.</blockquote><a href="/state/yemen-arab-republic">Yemen Arab Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Qa'atabah District, in what is now <a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaa">Sanaa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.91,44.63&amp;year=1965" aria-label="Qa'atabah District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ibrahim al-Hamdi"><image href="/maps/globes/g03000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6201 3822h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6201 3822h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.91,44.63&amp;year=1965">What was here in 1965? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
